expect: written
note: written
keeps: specification
keeps: both | either | supplemental
bars: \bdeclarescurrency\b
why: a reader who has not read the clearing specification cannot know why the currency is matched against two attributes; run 16's ruling kept that note on the function matching a content type against both, and the take-it-for-granted rule drops only a second place the code's own neighbours show, never a specification rule the reader has not read
labelled: 2026-09-29
--- code
export function declaresCurrency(posting: Posting, currency: string): boolean {
  return [posting.currency, posting.supplementalCurrencies].some((value) => value?.split(',').includes(currency));
}
--- facts
Under the clearing specification, `currency` may name only a base currency, and the supplemental currencies attribute then names the currency that extends it.
